\DOC X86_SIM_TAC

\TYPE {X86_SIM_TAC : ?preprocess_tac:tactic -> ?canonicalize_pc_diff:bool -> thm * (thm option array) -> int list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Symbolically executes the numbered x86 instruction steps of an {ensures}-style
machine-code goal, discharging the precondition and frame conditions and
leaving only the residual arithmetic/memory side conditions of the
postcondition.

\DESCRIBE
{X86_SIM_TAC} takes {preprocess_tac : tactic}, an optional tactic run on the
goal before symbolic execution starts; {canonicalize_pc_diff : bool}, an
optional flag controlling normalisation of program-counter differences; {execth
: thm * (thm option array)}, the execution/decoding data for the program being
simulated paired with a theorem giving the byte length of the program (its
statement appears in the observed traces); and {snums : int list}, the step
numbers to simulate, given as a range or an explicit list, which fixes how many
instructions are executed and the names of the intermediate states. Applied to
a goal whose conclusion is an {ensures} statement, it introduces an initial
state and a final state after the requested number of steps, turning the
precondition conjuncts into new assumptions {A'} about that final state
(register and flag reads, {bytes_loaded}, memory contents) together with one
{MAYCHANGE ... s0 sn} assumption recording the frame, and replaces the
conclusion by the leftover conjuncts {u} of the postcondition that symbolic
execution could not settle:
{
           A ?- t
     ================================  X86_SIM_TAC preprocess_tac canonicalize_pc_diff execth snums
           A, A' ?- u
}
\noindent In some applications a few of the original assumptions are also
deleted and re-added, reordered or re-instantiated, and in some cases the
final-state {RIP} assumption is left as a conditional branch target. When
symbolic execution settles the whole postcondition it produces no subgoals,
closing the goal.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only these two outcomes, always on goals whose
conclusion is an {ensures} statement for an x86 program.

\ENDDOC
